Building a functional 3D scene relies on three primary data management 
components.&]
[s22;:2`.1:%- 2.1. Model Containers and Geometry Management&]
[s5;%- Geometry is stored and organized within dedicated 3D mesh 
containers ([C^topic`:`/`/Sculptor`/src`/Upp`_Sculptor`_Model3D`_en`-us`#Upp`:`:Model3D^ M
odel3D]). These containers hold all vertex data, including spatial 
positions, surface normals, texture coordinates, and vertex colors, 
as well as index lists defining the triangular topology of the 
3D objects.&]
[s22;:2`.2:%- 2.2. Texture Mapping and Stream Rasterization&]
[s5;%- Surface detailing is achieved by binding bitmap textures to 
the model containers. The engine integrates with native stream 
rasterization utilities to load common image formats from package 
data files into memory, assigning each loaded asset a unique 
index identifier that links fragments to their visual data during 
the rasterization phase.&]
[s22;:2`.3:%- 2.3. Lighting and Shading Pipeline&]
[s5;%- To provide volumetric depth, the renderer accepts directional 
light sources into its scene environment. During the rasterization 
phase, the engine evaluates the dot product between the light 
vector and each interpolated surface normal, applying smooth 
diffuse shading directly across the rasterized fragments to simulate 
physical illumination.&]
